\chapter{Conclusions and Future Work}
\label{cha:conclusions}

This document establishes the groundwork for the dissertation "A Model-Driven Framework for the Automated Generation of Dynamic Digital Twins in Manufacturing". The main goal is to address interoperability barriers hindering \gls{DT} adoption in Industry 4.0, namely the gap between static engineering data and dynamic runtime environments.

\section{Summary of Contributions}
This preparation phase yielded three key contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{State of the Art Analysis:} Literature review confirmed that despite \gls{AML} and \gls{AAS} dominance in their domains, automated bridging solutions remain scarce. Existing methods often depend on unscalable manual mapping.
    \item \textbf{System Architecture Design:} A technology-agnostic architecture was proposed. Positioning \gls{AAS} as middleware ensures the \gls{DT} becomes an interoperable service rather than a mere visual replica. Automating \gls{AML} to \gls{AAS} translation targets the identified gap.
    \item \textbf{Implementation Roadmap:} A work plan was defined, segmenting development from the backend interoperability service to the frontend visualization client.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Future Work}
Future work shifts from design to implementation and validation. As detailed in \Cref{cha:preliminary_work}, immediate steps include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Development of the \gls{AML}-to-\gls{AAS} Parser:} Implementing logic to extract hierarchy and attributes from engineering files for \gls{AAS} instantiation.
    \item \textbf{Construction of the Visualization Client:} Creating game engine scripts to dynamically spawn 3D assets using live \gls{AAS} data.
    \item \textbf{Closing the Loop:} Enabling bidirectional communication via \gls{MQTT} or \gls{HTTP}/\gls{REST} so the virtual environment reflects the physical asset's real-time state.
\end{itemize}

\section{Final Remarks}
The preparation phase validated the approach's feasibility. The selected technologies and architecture form a basis for a framework reducing the engineering effort for dynamic \glspl{DT}. Successful execution will support Industry 4.0 standardization, showing how distinct standards can synergize for autonomous systems.